## What this is for

When each essay is read by whichever teacher it was assigned to, a harsh teacher is not noise to be averaged away: it is a bias in the scores of the essays that teacher happened to read. [Many sources of error: generalizability theory](g-theory.qmd) summarized raters with a variance component, which answers a design question (how many raters?) but not a respondent's question: was I scored by a harsh rater, and what would my score have been otherwise? This lesson gives each rater parameters of their own inside an item response model: severity in the many-facet Rasch model, centrality and halo, the hierarchical rater model for several ratings of one response, and drift. In the cleverness ratings the raters differ modestly and adjusting changes little; in the essays, teachers differ by about half a point, and an expert benchmark lets us check the adjustment.

::: {.callout-tip appearance="simple"}
**Recall** from [Many sources of error: generalizability theory](g-theory.qmd): rater severity, $\sigma^2_r$, is error for absolute decisions and, when respondents have different raters, for ranking too. It was 2.1% of a rating's variance in the cleverness ratings and 9.7% in the essays. Problem 6 asked when severity should be estimated and adjusted for instead.
:::

## Goals

By the end of this lesson you should be able to:

- write the many-facet Rasch model, and say what a rater's severity is and what the rating design must provide to estimate it;
- fit the model with `TAM`, read a Wright map with a rater column, and adjust scores for the raters a respondent drew;
- tell severity, centrality and halo apart, and say what data would show each;
- explain why the hierarchical rater model gives extra ratings of one response less credit than the many-facet model does;
- say when a rater is better treated as a fixed parameter, a random effect or a variance component.

## Core ideas

### Which rater, and by how much?

G theory and a rater model can be fitted to the same ratings ([Sudweeks, Reeve & Bradshaw, 2004](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asw.2004.11.001)), and G theory can itself be written as an item response model ([Briggs & Wilson, 2007](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1745-3984.2007.00031.x)). They report different things. G theory says how large the rater component is, which is what you need to choose a design. A rater model says that rater 5 is 0.37 logits harsher than average, with a standard error, which is what you need to fix one respondent's score. When every respondent has the same raters, severity shifts everyone alike and the two agree on ranking; when raters vary across respondents, they part.

### The many-facet Rasch model

::: {.callout-tip appearance="simple"}
**Recall** from [The Rasch model](rasch.qmd) and [Models for polytomous responses](polytomous.qmd): the Rasch log-odds is $\theta_j - b_i$, persons and items share one scale (the Wright map), and the sum score is sufficient. The rating scale model ([Andrich, 1978](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02293814)) makes each step between adjacent categories a Rasch item, with the same steps for every item.
:::

Add a rater. For respondent $j$, task $i$ and rater $r$, the **many-facet Rasch model** ([Linacre, 1989](https://www.winsteps.com/a/Linacre-MFRM-book.pdf); [Eckes, 2011](https://doi.org/10.3726/978-3-653-04844-5)) writes the log-odds of category $k$ over $k-1$ as
$$\log\frac{\Pr(x_{ijr} = k)}{\Pr(x_{ijr} = k-1)} = \theta_j - b_i - \rho_r - \tau_k .$$
$\rho_r$ is the rater's **severity**: a harsh rater acts like a harder item. The steps $\tau_k$ are shared by every task and rater. Raters are a third **facet**, and go on the Wright map beside the tasks ([Engelhard, 1994](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1745-3984.1994.tb00436.x), for essays; [Eckes, 2005](https://doi.org/10.1207/s15434311laq0203_2), for writing and speaking tests).

The Rasch algebra carries over: given the raters a respondent drew, the sum of the ratings is sufficient, and the estimate solves "expected total = observed total". The expected total depends on the raters, so equal raw totals from different raters give different estimates. That is the adjustment. Its usual report is the **fair average**, the rating a respondent would be expected to get from an average rater on average tasks ([Linacre, Facets manual](https://www.winsteps.com/facetman/fairaverage.htm)).

The adjustment has a price in design: severity and ability are separated only if the raters are linked, their respondents overlapping directly or through a chain of raters.

#### Try it: one harsh rater

Two hundred simulated respondents do three tasks, rated 1–5 by some of five raters drawn at random; rater 5 is harsher by the amount set below. Left: the raw mean rating. Right: the model's estimate of $\theta$, with the severities known (as after calibration).

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
viewof hr_harsh = Inputs.range([0, 2], {value: 1, step: 0.1, label: "How much harsher rater 5 is (logits)"})
viewof hr_per = Inputs.radio([2, 3, 5], {value: 2, label: "Raters per respondent"})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
hr = harshRater({seed: 150, n: 200, harsh: hr_harsh, per: hr_per})
hr_plot = (y, label) => Plot.plot({
  width: 340, height: 300, x: {label: "True θ"}, y: {label},
  color: {domain: ["did not", "drew the harsh rater"], range: [palette.light, palette.contrast], legend: true},
  marks: [Plot.dot(hr.pts, {x: "theta", y, fill: "drew", r: 3, fillOpacity: 0.8})]
})
html`<div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:1rem">${hr_plot("raw", "Raw mean rating")}${hr_plot("est", "Model estimate of θ")}</div>`
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
md`${hr.nHarsh} of 200 respondents drew rater 5. Their average distance from others with the same θ, in SDs of each score: **${hr.gapRaw.toFixed(2)}** on the raw mean, **${hr.gapEst.toFixed(2)}** on the model's estimate.`
```

At the starting values, respondents who drew rater 5 sit 0.19 SDs lower on the raw mean than others with the same $\theta$; the model's estimate removes the gap (0.02). Push the harshness to 2 and the raw gap doubles, to 0.40. Give everyone all five raters and the gap is zero for both.

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
quiz({
  q: "Rater A scored only the essays from school 1 and rater B only those from school 2. School 2's essays average one point lower. What can a many-facet Rasch analysis conclude?",
  options: [
    {text: "Nothing about which is harsher: rater and school are confounded", correct: true, why: "No essay links the two raters, so a lower average from B could be B's severity or school 2's essays. Severity is estimable only through overlap, direct or through a chain of raters."},
    {text: "Rater B is one point harsher", correct: false, why: "That assumes the two schools' essays are equally good, which is the question. The model can't separate the two without essays both raters scored."},
    {text: "School 2's essays are one point weaker", correct: false, why: "That assumes the raters are equally severe. Without linking essays, either reading fits the data equally well."}
  ]
})
```

Should you adjust? That rests on three assumptions: the design is linked, severities differ by more than their standard errors, and a rater's severity is the same for every respondent. When scores go to individuals, raters vary across respondents, and those assumptions hold, I adjust for severity and say in the report that I did. When every respondent had the same raters, I report the raw mean, since the adjustment can't change the order.

### Beyond severity: centrality and halo

Severity is where a rater sits. **Centrality** is how a rater uses the scale: a central rater avoids the ends and piles ratings in the middle, which shows up as rater-specific steps pulled apart. **Halo**: a rater's overall impression colours every criterion, so criteria move together more than the performance does ([Thorndike, 1920](https://doi.org/10.1037/h0071663), described it; [Saal, Downey & Lahey, 1980](https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.88.2.413), review how halo and other rating errors have been defined and measured). Each is a question for the data, not a charge against a rater: highly correlated criteria may reflect essays whose qualities do go together.

#### Try it: a central rater

One rater's ratings over a population of respondents, next to a typical rater's. Severity moves the steps together; spread stretches them apart (above 1) or squeezes them (below 1).

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
viewof rd_sev = Inputs.range([-1.5, 1.5], {value: 0, step: 0.1, label: "Severity (logits)"})
viewof rd_spread = Inputs.range([0.5, 2], {value: 1.6, step: 0.05, label: "Spread of the steps"})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
rd = ratingDist({sev: rd_sev, spread: rd_spread})
Plot.plot({
  width: 640, height: 260, x: {label: null, axis: null}, fx: {label: "Rating"}, y: {label: "Proportion", grid: true},
  color: {domain: ["typical rater", "this rater"], range: [palette.light, palette.main], legend: true},
  marks: [Plot.barY(rd.rows, {fx: "rating", x: "rater", y: "p", fill: "rater"})]
})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
md`Typical rater: mean **${rd.typical.m.toFixed(2)}**, SD **${rd.typical.sd.toFixed(2)}**. This rater: mean **${rd.me.m.toFixed(2)}**, SD **${rd.me.sd.toFixed(2)}**.`
```

With the steps spread by 1.6, the rater gives a 3 to 48% of respondents (against 36%) and the SD falls from 0.98 to 0.77, while the mean barely moves (2.95 against 2.97). Set spread to 1 and severity to 0.8, and the mean falls to 2.55 instead. Centrality is invisible in a rater's mean, so one severity per rater misses it.

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
quiz({
  q: "A rater gives nearly every essay a 3 or a 4 on a 1–6 scale, and their average matches the other raters'. What does a many-facet Rasch model with one severity per rater and shared steps say about this rater?",
  options: [
    {text: "Average severity; the pattern needs rater-specific steps to show", correct: true, why: "A shared-steps model has only a location for each rater, and this rater's location is average. Giving each rater their own steps shows this rater's outer steps far apart."},
    {text: "Harsh, because they never give a 6", correct: false, why: "They never give a 1 either. Severity is a shift of the whole scale, and this rater's average isn't shifted."},
    {text: "Inconsistent, so they add a large person × rater variance", correct: false, why: "Centrality is a systematic squeeze, not noise. A central rater can order essays just as the others do while using fewer categories."}
  ]
})
```

### The hierarchical rater model: raters rate the response

::: {.callout-tip appearance="simple"}
**Recall** from [The Rasch model](rasch.qmd): responses are assumed **locally independent**, so once we know $\theta$, one response says nothing more about another.
:::

The many-facet model applies that assumption to ratings: five ratings of one response count as five items, and each extra rater shrinks the standard error of $\hat\theta$ as an extra item would. But the five raters read the same response. If the respondent had an off day on the rope task, all five see a weaker list.

The **hierarchical rater model** ([Patz, Junker, Johnson & Mariano, 2002](https://doi.org/10.3102/10769986027004341)) puts the response between respondent and raters. Each response has an **ideal rating** $\xi_{ij}$, the category a perfectly accurate rater would give, generated from $\theta_j$ by a polytomous item response model; each rater reports $\xi_{ij}$ with a severity and precision of their own; [DeCarlo, Kim and Johnson (2011)](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1745-3984.2011.00143.x) use a signal-detection model for that layer. More raters pin down $\xi_{ij}$, and $\xi_{ij}$ is one observation of $\theta_j$, so the information about $\theta_j$ from one response can never exceed what the ideal rating carries. [Wilson and Hoskens (2001)](https://doi.org/10.3102/10769986026003283) model the same dependence with a rater bundle model and argue that ignoring it understates measurement error.

#### Try it: more raters, same responses

A binary version of both models: ten items at difficulty 0, a respondent at $\theta = 0$, each ideal rating a Rasch item, and each rater reporting it correctly with the probability set below.

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
viewof hs_acc = Inputs.range([0.6, 0.99], {value: 0.8, step: 0.01, label: "Chance a rater reports the ideal rating"})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
hs = hrmSE({acc: hs_acc, nItems: 10, maxR: 20})
Plot.plot({
  width: 640, height: 300, x: {label: "Raters per response"}, y: {label: "SE of θ", domain: [0, 1.8], grid: true},
  color: {legend: true, range: [palette.main, palette.contrast]},
  marks: [Plot.line(hs.rows, {x: "R", y: "se", stroke: "model", strokeWidth: 2.5}),
          Plot.ruleY([hs.floor], {stroke: palette.guide, strokeDasharray: "4 3"})]
})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
md`With 20 raters: **${hs.rows.find(d => d.R === 20 && d.model.startsWith("hier")).se.toFixed(3)}** under the hierarchical rater model, **${hs.rows.find(d => d.R === 20 && d.model.startsWith("local")).se.toFixed(3)}** if the ratings were independent. The dashed line, **${hs.floor.toFixed(3)}**, is the SE if every ideal rating were known.`
```

With raters who are right 80% of the time, 20 raters give a standard error of 0.634 under the hierarchical model, a hair above the floor of 0.632 set by the ideal ratings, against 0.236 if the ratings were independent. With one rater the models agree. Sharper raters (0.95) reach the floor with five raters; nothing goes below it (Go deeper, below).

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Go deeper: why extra raters can't beat the ideal rating

**Assumptions:** one response to one item with ideal rating $\xi$, whose distribution $\Pr(\xi \mid \theta)$ depends on $\theta$; ratings $\mathbf{x} = (x_1, \dots, x_R)$ that depend on $\theta$ only through $\xi$, so $\Pr(\mathbf{x} \mid \xi, \theta) = \Pr(\mathbf{x} \mid \xi)$. Nothing is assumed about how the raters err.

**Fisher information as a variance.** The information about $\theta$ in data $Y$ is $I_Y(\theta) = \text{Var}\big(\partial_\theta \log \Pr(Y \mid \theta)\big)$, the variance of the score.

**The ratings' score is a conditional mean.** Since $\Pr(\mathbf{x} \mid \theta) = \sum_\xi \Pr(\xi \mid \theta)\Pr(\mathbf{x} \mid \xi)$,
$$\partial_\theta \log \Pr(\mathbf{x} \mid \theta) = \sum_\xi \frac{\Pr(\xi \mid \theta)\Pr(\mathbf{x} \mid \xi)}{\Pr(\mathbf{x} \mid \theta)}\,\partial_\theta \log \Pr(\xi \mid \theta) = E\big[S \,\big|\, \mathbf{x}\big], \qquad S = \partial_\theta \log \Pr(\xi \mid \theta).$$
The weights are the posterior probabilities of each $\xi$ given the ratings, so the ratings' score is the ideal rating's score averaged over what the ratings leave uncertain about $\xi$.

**A conditional mean has less variance.** By the law of total variance, $\text{Var}(S) = \text{Var}\big(E[S \mid \mathbf{x}]\big) + E\big[\text{Var}(S \mid \mathbf{x})\big] \ge \text{Var}\big(E[S \mid \mathbf{x}]\big)$. So
$$I_{\mathbf{x}}(\theta) \le I_\xi(\theta)$$
for any number of raters. Equality needs $\text{Var}(S \mid \mathbf{x}) = 0$: the ratings must reveal $\xi$ exactly, which is the limit as raters become many or perfect. $\blacksquare$

Under local independence the information from $R$ raters is instead $R$ times the information from one, which grows without bound. The two agree at $R = 1$ and part as $R$ grows, as in the widget. With several items, sum over items: the bound is the information in the ideal ratings, the test a perfect rater would have scored.
:::

The hierarchical model's item response layer can be multidimensional ([Nieto & Casabianca, 2019](https://doi.org/10.1111/jedm.12225)) or longitudinal ([Casabianca, Junker, Nieto & Bond, 2017](https://doi.org/10.1080/00273171.2017.1342202)). The many-facet model is a reasonable choice when each response gets one rating, or when the raters are the object of study (monitoring a scoring session), since its parameters are simple to read and it fits in seconds.

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
quiz({
  q: "A program has 100 raters score every response on a 20-item constructed-response test. Under the hierarchical rater model, what is the standard error of a respondent's $\\hat\\theta$ close to?",
  options: [
    {text: "The SE the test would have if a perfect rater had scored it", correct: true, why: "With 100 raters each ideal rating is nearly known, so the information is nearly that of the 20 ideal ratings. The raters can't add information the responses don't carry."},
    {text: "Close to zero: 2,000 ratings is a lot of data", correct: false, why: "That is what local independence would say. Under the hierarchical model the 100 ratings of one response are 100 looks at one ideal rating."},
    {text: "About the SE with one rater per response", correct: false, why: "One fallible rater leaves each ideal rating uncertain, so the SE is larger than the floor. More raters help until the ideal ratings are pinned down, and then stop helping."}
  ]
})
```

### Raters change, and raters are many

A severity is a parameter only if it stays put. Add time to the rater term and test it: [Myford and Wolfe (2009)](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1745-3984.2009.00088.x) give indices for raters whose accuracy or use of the scale changes during scoring. In two years of classroom-observation ratings of 458 teachers, [Casabianca, Lockwood and McCaffrey (2015)](https://doi.org/10.1177/0013164414539163) found drift that was very large in raters' first days and persisted for nearly two years, and the variability among raters grew rather than shrank. Their model is a G study with time trends: drift is a variance component and a rater parameter at once.

#### Try it: drift

Eight raters score for 30 days, each moving by a fixed amount per day: a shared drift plus a part of their own.

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
viewof dr_common = Inputs.range([-0.03, 0.03], {value: 0, step: 0.005, label: "Shared drift per day (logits)"})
viewof dr_spread = Inputs.range([0, 0.05], {value: 0.02, step: 0.005, label: "SD of raters' own drift per day"})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
dr = drift({seed: 2015, nr: 8, days: 30, common: dr_common, spread: dr_spread})
Plot.plot({
  width: 640, height: 280, x: {label: "Day of scoring"}, y: {label: "Severity (logits)", domain: [-1.8, 1.8], grid: true},
  marks: [Plot.line(dr.rows, {x: "day", y: "severity", z: "rater", stroke: palette.light, strokeWidth: 1.5}),
          Plot.line(dr.sds, {x: "day", y: "mean", stroke: palette.main, strokeWidth: 3})]
})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
md`SD of severity across raters: **${dr.sds[0].sd.toFixed(2)}** on day 1, **${dr.sds[29].sd.toFixed(2)}** on day 30. Mean severity (dark line): ${dr.sds[0].mean.toFixed(2)} on day 1, ${dr.sds[29].mean.toFixed(2)} on day 30.`
```

A shared drift moves the dark line and leaves the spread alone. Raters' own drift fans the lines out: at the starting values the SD across raters grows from 0.36 to 0.54, the pattern Casabianca et al. found, and a severity estimated on day 1 is out of date by day 30.

Two design points follow. First, raters must stay linked over time. A **linkage set**, responses scored by most or all raters, helps: [Casabianca, Donoghue, Shin, Chao and Choi (2023)](https://doi.org/10.1111/jedm.12360) found that a large one spanning the score scale improved connectedness, that two human ratings connected raters better than one human plus an automated engine, and that engine scores alone did not connect raters adequately. Second, with hundreds of raters rating a handful each, severities are better treated as **random effects**, shrunk toward the mean in proportion to how little each rater rated ([De Boeck, 2008](https://doi.org/10.1007/s11336-008-9092-x), does the same for items; fitted with `lme4`, [Bates, Mächler, Bolker & Walker, 2015](https://doi.org/10.18637/jss.v067.i01)). The random-effect variance is G theory's $\sigma^2_r$ again. [What is an item?](explanatory-irt.qmd) develops random effects in item response models, and [AI and psychometrics](ai-psychometrics.qmd) treats an automated scoring engine as one more rater.

## Simulate

The code simulates 200 respondents, three tasks and five raters, one harsh (rater 5) and one central (rater 4, steps stretched by 1.6), with two raters per respondent. It fits the many-facet Rasch model with `TAM` ([Robitzsch, Kiefer & Wu, 2025](https://doi.org/10.32614/CRAN.package.TAM)), then rater-specific steps, and compares raw means with the model's estimates. Loading `TAM` takes 20 to 30 seconds the first time; the fits take a second or two.

With the seed as written, the severities come back within 0.14 of the truth; rater 5's has the largest standard error (0.13), since `TAM` derives the last rater's value from the others'. Rater 4's first-to-last step span is 8.6 logits against 4.9 to 5.8 for the rest (truth 8.8 and 5.5). The raw mean and the estimate correlate about equally with $\theta$ (0.90 and 0.91), but respondents who drew rater 5 sit 0.17 SDs low on the raw mean and 0.02 on the estimate. Set `per <- 5` and both correlations rise to 0.96, with no gap. Try a harsher rater 5, or `spread` of 1 for everyone.


::: {.cell}
```{webr}
#| results: markup
#| warning: false
#| message: false
#| include: true
#| label: unnamed-chunk-19
# Simulate ratings from a many-facet Rasch model with one harsh rater and one
# central rater, fit the model with TAM (Robitzsch, Kiefer & Wu), and compare the
# estimates with the truth. Then give each respondent only some of the raters and
# see what adjusting for severity buys over the raw mean.
library(TAM)
set.seed(150)
np  <- 200                          # respondents
nt  <- 3                            # tasks
per <- 2                            # raters per respondent (5 = fully crossed)
theta <- rnorm(np, 0, 1.2)          # respondents' levels, in logits
delta <- c(-0.2, 0, 0.2)            # task difficulties
sev   <- c(-0.3, -0.2, 0, -0.1, 0.6)  # rater severities: rater 5 is harsh
tau   <- c(-2.8, -0.7, 0.8, 2.7)    # steps between categories 0-1, ..., 3-4
spread <- c(1, 1, 1, 1.6, 1)        # rater 4 stretches the steps apart: central

# Rating scale model with a rater facet: the log-odds of category k over k - 1
# is theta - delta - severity - tau_k (rater 4's taus are stretched).
rate <- function(th, d, r) {
  eta <- cumsum(th - d - sev[r] - tau * spread[r])
  p <- exp(c(0, eta)); sample(0:4, 1, prob = p / sum(p))
}
rows <- do.call(rbind, lapply(1:np, function(j) {
  raters <- sort(sample(1:5, per))
  t(sapply(raters, function(r) c(id = j, rater = r, sapply(1:nt, function(i) rate(theta[j], delta[i], r)))))
}))
d <- as.data.frame(rows)
names(d)[3:(2 + nt)] <- paste0("task", 1:nt)
resp <- d[, 3:(2 + nt)]

# Many-facet Rasch model: task + rater + common steps. TAM reports severities that
# sum to zero, so compare them with the centred truth.
# (TAM warns about row names when it builds the design matrices; the warnings are harmless.)
fit <- suppressWarnings(tam.mml.mfr(resp, facets = d["rater"], pid = d$id,
                                    formulaA = ~ item + rater + step, verbose = FALSE))
est <- fit$xsi.facets
round(cbind(true = sev - mean(sev), estimate = est$xsi[est$facet == "rater"],
            se = est$se.xsi[est$facet == "rater"]), 2)

# Rater-specific steps: a central rater's outer steps sit further out.
fit2 <- suppressWarnings(tam.mml.mfr(resp, facets = d["rater"], pid = d$id,
                                     formulaA = ~ item + rater + step + rater:step, verbose = FALSE))
x2 <- fit2$xsi.facets
dev <- x2[x2$facet == "step:rater", ]
step_of <- as.integer(sub("step(\\d):.*", "\\1", dev$parameter))
rater_of <- sub(".*:rater", "", dev$parameter)
common <- setNames(x2$xsi[x2$facet == "step"], 1:4)
# Distance from each rater's first step to their last: wider = more central.
span <- tapply(dev$xsi[step_of == 4], rater_of[step_of == 4], sum) -
        tapply(dev$xsi[step_of == 1], rater_of[step_of == 1], sum) + common[4] - common[1]
round(span, 2)
round(c(common_steps_AIC = fit$ic$AIC, rater_steps_AIC = fit2$ic$AIC))

# Scores: the raw mean of each respondent's ratings against the model's estimate
# (weighted likelihood), each correlated with the true theta.
wle <- tam.wle(fit, progress = FALSE)
raw <- tapply(rowMeans(resp), d$id, mean)
drew_harsh <- tapply(d$rater == 5, d$id, any)
round(c(raw = cor(raw, theta), model = cor(wle$theta, theta)), 3)
# How far respondents who drew the harsh rater sit below others with the same true
# theta, in SDs of each score (raw means are in rating points, estimates in logits):
gap <- function(s) mean(resid(lm(s ~ theta))[drew_harsh]) / sd(s)
round(c(raw = gap(raw), model = gap(wle$theta)), 2)

